<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Scheveningen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Scheveningen is a seaside district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, originally a fishing village on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> coast. On 30 November 1813, after the collapse of French rule, William Frederick of Orange-Nassau landed on its beach to return from exile and accept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, becoming in 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-of-the-netherlands">King William I of the Netherlands</a>. In the nineteenth century it developed into the country's best-known beach resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/scheveningen.jpg" alt="Scheveningen" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zairon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Den_Haag_Scheveningen_Pier_32.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
